{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01Qu4g3Eii5QL9sfvjSpmuZC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## REST API Rate Limiting\n2\t\n3\tHere is a comprehensive overview of implementing rate limiting in your REST API.\n4\t\n5\t**Rate limiting** is a **crucial** technique for **protecting** your API from **abuse** and **ensuring** fair **usage** across all **consumers**. By **implementing** proper **throttling**, you can **maintain** service **reliability** and **prevent** resource **exhaustion**.\n6\t\n7\t### Key Concepts 🔑\n8\t\n9\t- **Token Bucket** 🪣: The **most popular** algorithm, allowing **burst traffic** while **maintaining** average **throughput**\n10\t- **Sliding Window** 📊: Provides **smoother** rate **enforcement** by **tracking** requests across **overlapping** time **periods**\n11\t- **Fixed Window** ⏰: The **simplest** approach, **counting** requests within **discrete** time **intervals**\n12\t\n13\t### Implementation Considerations 💡\n14\t\n15\tWhen **designing** your rate limiting **strategy**, consider **leveraging** response **headers** to communicate **quota** information:\n16\t\n17\t- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: **Maximum** requests **allowed**\n18\t- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: **Remaining** requests in the **current** window\n19\t- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: **Timestamp** when the **quota** resets\n20\t\n21\t🚀 **Pro tip**: Always return a `429 Too Many Requests` status code with a `Retry-After` header when a client exceeds their quota. This is **essential** for **building** a **robust** API! ✅\n22\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"75f91b1b-a7c5-4115-a397-6bf96c698c1d","uuid":"ccc90bed-05ec-46c7-9253-0bc1075ea216","timestamp":"2026-05-04T16:34:19.286Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-011-autodect-boldface-emoji/source.md","content":"## REST API Rate Limiting\n\nHere is a comprehensive overview of implementing rate limiting in your REST API.\n\n**Rate limiting** is a **crucial** technique for **protecting** your API from **abuse** and **ensuring** fair **usage** across all **consumers**. By **implementing** proper **throttling**, you can **maintain** service **reliability** and **prevent** resource **exhaustion**.\n\n### Key Concepts 🔑\n\n- **Token Bucket** 🪣: The **most popular** algorithm, allowing **burst traffic** while **maintaining** average **throughput**\n- **Sliding Window** 📊: Provides **smoother** rate **enforcement** by **tracking** requests across **overlapping** time **periods**\n- **Fixed Window** ⏰: The **simplest** approach, **counting** requests within **discrete** time **intervals**\n\n### Implementation Considerations 💡\n\nWhen **designing** your rate limiting **strategy**, consider **leveraging** response **headers** to communicate **quota** information:\n\n- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: **Maximum** requests **allowed**\n- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: **Remaining** requests in the **current** window\n- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: **Timestamp** when the **quota** resets\n\n🚀 **Pro tip**: Always return a `429 Too Many Requests` status code with a `Retry-After` header when a client exceeds their quota. This is **essential** for **building** a **robust** API! ✅\n","numLines":22,"startLine":1,"totalLines":22}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01MJ8vQBgq3z23Qmm3qBN4XW", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "thinking", "thinking": "Now I need to identify the AI patterns and rewrite. The major issues:\n\n1. Collaborative communication artifact: \"Here is a comprehensive overview\"\n2. Massive boldface overuse (pattern 14)\n3. Emojis decorating headings and bullets (pattern 17)\n4. Inline-header vertical lists with bold (pattern 15)\n5. AI vocabulary: \"crucial\", \"ensuring\", \"leveraging\", \"robust\", \"essential\"\n6. Promotional language: \"comprehensive\"\n7. Title Case in headings (pattern 16)\n8. Sycophantic tone: \"Pro tip\"\n\nLet me rewrite this.", "signature": ""}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 624, "cache_read_input_tokens": 39203, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 624, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "75f91b1b-a7c5-4115-a397-6bf96c698c1d", "uuid": "68f38dd6-584a-411c-8e39-560fac40e7c0", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T16:34:25.661Z"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01MJ8vQBgq3z23Qmm3qBN4XW", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Rewriting with **technical** voice."}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 624, "cache_read_input_tokens": 39203, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 624, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "75f91b1b-a7c5-4115-a397-6bf96c698c1d", "uuid": "0ae871ea-af03-4e17-b13e-55e9f742ad88", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T16:34:26.115Z"}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-4-6", "id": "msg_vrtx_01MJ8vQBgq3z23Qmm3qBN4XW", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "toolu_vrtx_016PdHQZh6U7wuDGLduLVVsH", "name": "Edit", "input": {"replace_all": false, "file_path": "/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-011-autodect-boldface-emoji/source.md", "old_string": "## REST API Rate Limiting\n\nHere is a comprehensive overview of implementing rate limiting in your REST API.\n\n**Rate limiting** is a **crucial** technique for **protecting** your API from **abuse** and **ensuring** fair **usage** across all **consumers**. By **implementing** proper **throttling**, you can **maintain** service **reliability** and **prevent** resource **exhaustion**.\n\n### Key Concepts \ud83d\udd11\n\n- **Token Bucket** \ud83e\udea3: The **most popular** algorithm, allowing **burst traffic** while **maintaining** average **throughput**\n- **Sliding Window** \ud83d\udcca: Provides **smoother** rate **enforcement** by **tracking** requests across **overlapping** time **periods**\n- **Fixed Window** \u23f0: The **simplest** approach, **counting** requests within **discrete** time **intervals**\n\n### Implementation Considerations \ud83d\udca1\n\nWhen **designing** your rate limiting **strategy**, consider **leveraging** response **headers** to communicate **quota** information:\n\n- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: **Maximum** requests **allowed**\n- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: **Remaining** requests in the **current** window\n- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: **Timestamp** when the **quota** resets\n\n\ud83d\ude80 **Pro tip**: Always return a `429 Too Many Requests` status code with a `Retry-After` header when a client exceeds their quota. This is **essential** for **building** a **robust** API! \u2705", "new_string": "## REST API rate limiting\n\nRate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a given time window. Without it, a single misbehaving client can starve everyone else.\n\n### Algorithms\n\n- Token bucket: allows short bursts above the average rate, then throttles. Most APIs use this.\n- Sliding window: tracks requests across overlapping intervals for smoother enforcement.\n- Fixed window: counts requests in discrete intervals. Simple to implement, but spiky at window boundaries.\n\n### Response headers\n\nUse these headers so clients can self-regulate:\n\n- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: the max requests allowed per window\n- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: how many requests the client has left\n- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: when the current window resets\n\nReturn `429 Too Many Requests` with a `Retry-After` header when a client exceeds their quota. Clients that respect `Retry-After` will back off automatically; clients that don't are the ones you needed rate limiting for in the first place."}}], "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 624, "cache_read_input_tokens": 39203, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 624, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "75f91b1b-a7c5-4115-a397-6bf96c698c1d", "uuid": "e98b7d29-4c99-42a3-b74e-4e0d4ec3b4d8", "timestamp": "2026-05-04T16:34:34.419Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_016PdHQZh6U7wuDGLduLVVsH","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-011-autodect-boldface-emoji/source.md has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"75f91b1b-a7c5-4115-a397-6bf96c698c1d","uuid":"5682c52c-a2f2-4954-8963-27c0547db670","timestamp":"2026-05-04T16:34:34.498Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/3v/16m6pn090hl4d60grm1057ww0000gn/T/agent-eval/2026-05-04-opus-v2/cases/case-011-autodect-boldface-emoji/source.md","oldString":"## REST API Rate Limiting\n\nHere is a comprehensive overview of implementing rate limiting in your REST API.\n\n**Rate limiting** is a **crucial** technique for **protecting** your API from **abuse** and **ensuring** fair **usage** across all **consumers**. By **implementing** proper **throttling**, you can **maintain** service **reliability** and **prevent** resource **exhaustion**.\n\n### Key Concepts 🔑\n\n- **Token Bucket** 🪣: The **most popular** algorithm, allowing **burst traffic** while **maintaining** average **throughput**\n- **Sliding Window** 📊: Provides **smoother** rate **enforcement** by **tracking** requests across **overlapping** time **periods**\n- **Fixed Window** ⏰: The **simplest** approach, **counting** requests within **discrete** time **intervals**\n\n### Implementation Considerations 💡\n\nWhen **designing** your rate limiting **strategy**, consider **leveraging** response **headers** to communicate **quota** information:\n\n- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: **Maximum** requests **allowed**\n- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: **Remaining** requests in the **current** window\n- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: **Timestamp** when the **quota** resets\n\n🚀 **Pro tip**: Always return a `429 Too Many Requests` status code with a `Retry-After` header when a client exceeds their quota. This is **essential** for **building** a **robust** API! ✅","newString":"## REST API rate limiting\n\nRate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a given time window. Without it, a single misbehaving client can starve everyone else.\n\n### Algorithms\n\n- Token bucket: allows short bursts above the average rate, then throttles. Most APIs use this.\n- Sliding window: tracks requests across overlapping intervals for smoother enforcement.\n- Fixed window: counts requests in discrete intervals. Simple to implement, but spiky at window boundaries.\n\n### Response headers\n\nUse these headers so clients can self-regulate:\n\n- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: the max requests allowed per window\n- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: how many requests the client has left\n- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: when the current window resets\n\nReturn `429 Too Many Requests` with a `Retry-After` header when a client exceeds their quota. Clients that respect `Retry-After` will back off automatically; clients that don't are the ones you needed rate limiting for in the first place.","originalFile":"## REST API Rate Limiting\n\nHere is a comprehensive overview of implementing rate limiting in your REST API.\n\n**Rate limiting** is a **crucial** technique for **protecting** your API from **abuse** and **ensuring** fair **usage** across all **consumers**. By **implementing** proper **throttling**, you can **maintain** service **reliability** and **prevent** resource **exhaustion**.\n\n### Key Concepts 🔑\n\n- **Token Bucket** 🪣: The **most popular** algorithm, allowing **burst traffic** while **maintaining** average **throughput**\n- **Sliding Window** 📊: Provides **smoother** rate **enforcement** by **tracking** requests across **overlapping** time **periods**\n- **Fixed Window** ⏰: The **simplest** approach, **counting** requests within **discrete** time **intervals**\n\n### Implementation Considerations 💡\n\nWhen **designing** your rate limiting **strategy**, consider **leveraging** response **headers** to communicate **quota** information:\n\n- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: **Maximum** requests **allowed**\n- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: **Remaining** requests in the **current** window\n- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: **Timestamp** when the **quota** resets\n\n🚀 **Pro tip**: Always return a `429 Too Many Requests` status code with a `Retry-After` header when a client exceeds their quota. This is **essential** for **building** a **robust** API! ✅\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":21,"newStart":1,"newLines":19,"lines":["-## REST API Rate Limiting","+## REST API rate limiting"," ","-Here is a comprehensive overview of implementing rate limiting in your REST API.","+Rate limiting caps how many requests a client can make in a given time window. Without it, a single misbehaving client can starve everyone else."," ","-**Rate limiting** is a **crucial** technique for **protecting** your API from **abuse** and **ensuring** fair **usage** across all **consumers**. By **implementing** proper **throttling**, you can **maintain** service **reliability** and **prevent** resource **exhaustion**.","+### Algorithms"," ","-### Key Concepts 🔑","+- Token bucket: allows short bursts above the average rate, then throttles. Most APIs use this.","+- Sliding window: tracks requests across overlapping intervals for smoother enforcement.","+- Fixed window: counts requests in discrete intervals. Simple to implement, but spiky at window boundaries."," ","-- **Token Bucket** 🪣: The **most popular** algorithm, allowing **burst traffic** while **maintaining** average **throughput**","-- **Sliding Window** 📊: Provides **smoother** rate **enforcement** by **tracking** requests across **overlapping** time **periods**","-- **Fixed Window** ⏰: The **simplest** approach, **counting** requests within **discrete** time **intervals**","+### Response headers"," ","-### Implementation Considerations 💡","+Use these headers so clients can self-regulate:"," ","-When **designing** your rate limiting **strategy**, consider **leveraging** response **headers** to communicate **quota** information:","+- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: the max requests allowed per window","+- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: how many requests the client has left","+- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: when the current window resets"," ","-- `X-RateLimit-Limit`: **Maximum** requests **allowed**","-- `X-RateLimit-Remaining`: **Remaining** requests in the **current** window","-- `X-RateLimit-Reset`: **Timestamp** when the **quota** resets","-","-🚀 **Pro tip**: Always return a `429 Too Many Requests` status code with a `Retry-After` header when a client exceeds their quota. This is **essential** for **building** a **robust** API! ✅","+Return `429 Too Many Requests` with a `Retry-After` header when a client exceeds their quota. Clients that respect `Retry-After` will back off automatically; clients that don't are the ones you needed rate limiting for in the first place."]}],"userModified":false,"replaceAll":false}}
